  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 1121 PM MDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nTonight's temperatures will dip into the lower 50s for the plains \nwith mid 20s to upper 30s in the mountains and valleys. There is \nalso the chance for some patchy fog/low clouds Saturday morning \nmainly from Limon southward. Saturday will be warm and dry as the \nridge builds overhead. It will be breezy in the plains, 20-30 kts, \nas a weak cold front pushes through during the day. Due to these \ngusty and dry conditions, elevated fire weather conditions are \nexpected for a few hours in the afternoon near the Cheyenne Ridge \narea with the passage of the front. Sunday will see a slight drop in \ntemperatures due to the passage of the front, but still above normal \nin the upper 70s and low 80s is likely for most of the urban \ncorridor and plains. An isolated shower/storm in the high county \ncan't be ruled out.\n\nMonday temperatures heat up again to near record for Denver. Current \nforecasted temperature is 86 and that would put us just one degree \nshy from the daily record set in 2024 at 87 degrees. Elsewhere, we \nshould reach the mid/upper 80s with Greeley nearing 90 degrees. A \nweak disturbance in the ridge is still on track to push across the \nstate Tuesday. We currently have a 20-30% for an isolated shower or \nweak storm across the high country. With a focus on the Central \nMountains, such as Clear Creek and Summit Counties. Ensemble \nguidance is in great agreement for PWATs in the plains to be fairly \nlow (0.3-0.5), but with some forcing from the shortwave we may see a \nfew showers attempt to make it into the Palmer Divide area. \n\nWednesday through Friday deterministic and ensemble guidance \nmaintain great agreement for a upper level ridging pattern to \nsettle in. This means our temperatures should stay above normal in\nthe low/mid 80s through the end of next week with dry weather.",
